Mechanism:
- The neighboring provider_gui restart test previously failed after successful Supervisor::shutdown with AddrInUse / ownership flock EWOULDBLOCK (35). Its original holder PID was not sampled; that identity remains UNPROVEN.
- Public Supervisor probe at fffc95e5: ten ordinary shutdown/restart controls passed. With a controlled fork child retaining the ownership descriptor, shutdown closed the parent's original fd (F_GETFD -> EBADF (9)), yet restart failed with the same flock error. lsof independently identified the child holding the lock file. Reaping it immediately restored restart. Child 77403 reaped status 0.
- CLOEXEC closes at exec, not at fork. A descriptor inherited during the native launch interval can therefore extend the open-file-description flock past owner shutdown. Parent actor joining was already correct. This acquisition/release branch predates #263 (ab4ef00e); #263 left it unchanged.

Change, exact scope:
- crates/bbt-supervisor/src/transport.rs:317 — after unlinking its own socket inode, BoundSocket::Drop explicitly LOCK_UNs its valid ownership fd before File close. EINTR retries; other unlock errors are logged once. The persistent lock inode, startup validation, live-owner/connect checks and O_CLOEXEC remain.
- transport.rs:813 — deterministic two-phase fork/gate regression. The child reports that the inherited CLOEXEC fd remains valid both before owner drop and after the replacement bind attempt. Replacement must succeed before child release/reap; the lock inode stays the same; the replacement's live ownership still refuses a second bind without changing its socket inode. The test reaps before assertions and has no sleeps or wall-clock success criterion.

